.TH NETPIPE-PYTHON 7 "June 2026" "netpipe v0.1.0" "Miscellaneous"
.SH NAME
netpipe-python \- interfacing netpipe with Python using JSON streams
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B netpipe
utility provides an elegant, zero-dependency interface for Python applications.
Instead of loading heavy, complex binary bindings (e.g. ctypes or C extensions), Python scripts can execute
.B netpipe
as a subprocess with the
.B \-fmt json
option.
.PP
In this mode,
.B netpipe
emits one JSON-formatted packet per line (Newline-Delimited JSON / NDJSON) to its standard output.
The Python application reads this stream line-by-line using standard subprocess and JSON libraries.
.SH JSON SCHEMA
Each line written by
.B netpipe \-fmt json
is a single JSON object representing a captured packet.
.SS Standard Fields
All packet objects contain the following top-level keys:
.TP
.B seq
(integer) A 1-indexed global packet capture sequence number.
.TP
.B ts
(string) The packet arrival timestamp formatted as `HH:MM:SS.microseconds` (e.g., "14:20:05.123456").
.TP
.B caplen
(integer) The number of bytes captured.
.TP
.B wirelen
(integer) The original packet size as seen on the physical wire.
.TP
.B flow_id
(integer) A unique 32-bit hash signature computed from the packet's 5-tuple (source IP, destination IP, source port, destination port, and protocol).
Packets belonging to the same bidirectional conversation share this ID.
.TP
.B layers
(array of objects) The stack of network layers parsed from the packet. Each object contains:
.RS
.IP "proto" 8
(string) The protocol identifier. Supported protocol strings include:
.I "ethernet", "arp", "ipv4", "ipv6", "icmp", "tcp", "udp", "dns", "http", "tls".
.IP "len" 8
(integer) The size of this layer's header + payload in bytes.
.RE
.TP
.B raw_hex
(string) A hexadecimal representation of the raw captured frame bytes.
.SS Decoded DNS Fields
If the packet contains DNS traffic (UDP/TCP port 53) and was parsed successfully, a top-level
.B dns
dictionary is present:
.TP
.B is_response
(boolean)
.B false
if the packet is a query;
.B true
if it is a response.
.TP
.B query
(object, present in queries) Contains:
.RS
.IP "name" 8
(string) The queried domain name (e.g. "example.com").
.IP "type" 8
(integer) The DNS query type number (e.g. 1 for A, 28 for AAAA, 5 for CNAME).
.RE
.TP
.B answers
(array of objects, present in responses) Each answer record contains:
.RS
.IP "name" 8
(string) The domain name.
.IP "data" 8
(string) The resolved value (e.g., an IPv4 address, IPv6 address, or CNAME target).
.RE
.SS Decoded HTTP Fields
If the packet contains HTTP/1.1 traffic (port 80 or heuristic matches) and was parsed successfully, a top-level
.B http
dictionary is present:
.TP
.B method
(string, request only) The HTTP request method (e.g. "GET", "POST").
.TP
.B path
(string, request only) The requested resource URI path (e.g. "/index.html").
.TP
.B status
(integer, response only) The HTTP response status code (e.g. 200, 404).
.TP
.B phrase
(string, response only) The HTTP response status phrase (e.g. "OK", "Not Found").
.TP
.B headers
(object) A dictionary of key-value pairs representing the HTTP headers.
.SS Reassembled Stream Fields
If the TCP stream reassembly processor is enabled (via
.B \-proc tcp-stream\fR),
the packet may contain:
.TP
.B stream_hex
(string) A hexadecimal representation of the cumulative reassembled TCP stream bytes for the packet's flow up to this point.
.SH PYTHON RECIPES
The following script template demonstrates how to launch
.B netpipe
as a subprocess and parse packets in real time:
.PP
.nf
import subprocess
import json
import sys

# Define netpipe command line
cmd = ["netpipe", "-i", "eth0", "-fmt", "json", "-q"]

# Launch subprocess
proc = subprocess.Popen(
    cmd,
    stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
    stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
    text=True
)

try:
    for line in proc.stdout:
        line = line.strip()
        if not line:
            continue
        
        # Parse packet dictionary
        pkt = json.loads(line)
        
        # Check for HTTP request
        http = pkt.get("http")
        if http and "method" in http:
            print(f"[{pkt['ts']}] {http['method']} {http['path']}")
            
except KeyboardInterrupt:
    proc.terminate()
    print("\\nStopped.")
.fi
